<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<bpmn:definitions xmlns:bpmn="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/MODEL" xmlns:bpmndi="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/DI" xmlns:dc="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DC" xmlns:di="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DI" xmlns:spiffworkflow="http://spiffworkflow.org/bpmn/schema/1.0/core" id="Definitions_OrchestratorAgent" targetNamespace="http://bpmn.io/schema/bpmn">
  <bpmn:process id="orchestrator_agent" name="Orchestrator Agent" isExecutable="true">
    <bpmn:documentation>The agent the Software Development process hands a Work Item to when `orchestrator` is ticked instead of assigning it to a person. It reads the brief off the Work Item, decides which specialist can build the thing, and delegates over A2A — each specialist is a shape in the Tools sub-process, so widening the roster is a shape, not a code change. It runs as a sub-process of the caller, so the Work Item is already the instance context and the report goes back as workflow data.</bpmn:documentation>
    <bpmn:startEvent id="start" name="Orchestration requested">
      <bpmn:documentation>Started by the Call Activity in the Software Development process when a Work Item has `orchestrator` ticked. Deliberately a plain start event: a called process is started by its caller, so it carries no trigger of its own.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f1</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:startEvent>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f1" sourceRef="start" targetRef="read_brief" />
    <bpmn:scriptTask id="read_brief" name="Read the work item" spiffworkflow:serverScript="Orchestrator Agent: Read the Work Item" spiffworkflow:scriptType="Server Script" spiffworkflow:scriptName="Orchestrator Agent: Read the Work Item">
      <bpmn:documentation>Builds the agent's brief from the Work Item that is this instance's context — the same title and description a person would have read.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f1</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f2</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:scriptTask>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f2" sourceRef="read_brief" targetRef="gw_merge" />
    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="gw_merge">
      <bpmn:documentation>Where the Tools sub-process returns. The agent task runs its own tool loop internally, so in practice nothing comes back this way — the loop exists so the toolbox is part of the diagram rather than floating beside it.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f2</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:incoming>f_tools_back</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f3</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f3" sourceRef="gw_merge" targetRef="orchestrate" />
    <bpmn:serviceTask id="orchestrate" name="Decide and delegate" spiffworkflow:serviceType="ai_agent" spiffworkflow:aiBackend="direct_api" spiffworkflow:aiProvider="Anthropic" spiffworkflow:aiModel="Orchestrator Agent - claude-sonnet-5-5" spiffworkflow:aiOutputVariable="ai_result" spiffworkflow:aiSystemPrompt="You are the Orchestrator. The Software Development process hands you a work item that was flagged for you instead of being assigned to a person, and you are responsible for getting it built.&#10;&#10;You do not build anything yourself. You have specialist agents as tools, one per kind of work, and your job is to read the brief, decide which specialist can do it, and hand them a clear instruction.&#10;&#10;Work in this order.&#10;&#10;1. Read the work item brief and decide what actually has to be built.&#10;2. If the brief says the work item is in Changes Requested and names a pull request, call get_pull_request FIRST. The review comments - above all the ones that asked for changes - are the whole job: the specialist must fix exactly what they ask and nothing else, so your instruction names each comment (file, line, what the reviewer wants) and says the fix goes back onto the same pull request.&#10;3. If one of your specialist tools covers the work, call it with a clear instruction. The specialist can read the work item and the pull request itself, so the instruction is your framing - what to build or fix and any decision you took - not a copy of the brief. The target app and branch are fields on the work item and appear in the brief header; the delegation carries them to the specialist as they are. Never infer, correct or restate them from the description - if the header says one is not set, say so in your instruction rather than guessing.&#10;4. If nothing you have covers it, do not guess and do not call a tool. Say plainly what the work item needs and that no specialist is available for it.&#10;&#10;A specialist may take a while, and it may come back reporting that it stopped short. Report what it actually said - never claim work was completed that the specialist did not confirm.&#10;&#10;Finish with a short plain-language report: what you decided, who you delegated to, and what came back. No preamble, no bullet points, no invented next steps.&#10;&#10;Briefs that cannot be built as written:&#10;- A brief with no description, or a title alone, goes back with wi_hand_back asking for a proper brief. Do not invent requirements from the title, and do not ask a question - one question cannot make a brief out of nothing.&#10;- A brief that contradicts itself is not resolved by you. Name each contradiction in the reason of the hand-back or in the question, and do not pick a reading.&#10;- The report is plain sentences only: no headings, no bold, no horizontal rules, no bullet or numbered lists, no markdown of any kind, and no links you did not receive from a tool.&#10;" spiffworkflow:aiUserPrompt="{{ work_item_brief }}" spiffworkflow:aiResponseFormat="text" spiffworkflow:aiTemperature="0.2" spiffworkflow:aiTopP="1" spiffworkflow:aiMaxTokens="32768" spiffworkflow:aiTimeout="160" spiffworkflow:aiMaxRetries="2" spiffworkflow:aiAgentConfig="Orchestrator Agent" spiffworkflow:aiToolsAdhoc="orchestrator_tools" spiffworkflow:aiMaxToolCalls="8">
      <bpmn:documentation>Chooses a specialist and delegates to it over A2A. The tools are the shapes of the Tools sub-process below.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f3</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f4</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:serviceTask>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f4" sourceRef="orchestrate" targetRef="gw_needs_tools" />
    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="gw_needs_tools" name="Needs to call tools?" default="f5">
      <bpmn:documentation>Decorative on purpose. The AI Agent Task runs its tool loop inside itself, so "Read the work item" pins needs_tools to False and this always takes the No branch. It is here so the Tools sub-process is wired into the diagram.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f4</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f_call_tools</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f5</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f_call_tools" name="Call tools" sourceRef="gw_needs_tools" targetRef="orchestrator_tools">
      <bpmn:conditionExpression>needs_tools == True</bpmn:conditionExpression>
    </bpmn:sequenceFlow>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f5" name="No" sourceRef="gw_needs_tools" targetRef="answer" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f_tools_back" name="Tool call results" sourceRef="orchestrator_tools" targetRef="gw_merge" />
    <bpmn:scriptTask id="answer" name="Record the outcome" spiffworkflow:serverScript="Orchestrator Agent: Record the Outcome" spiffworkflow:scriptType="Server Script" spiffworkflow:scriptName="Orchestrator Agent: Record the Outcome">
      <bpmn:documentation>Hands the report back to the caller as workflow data and records it on the Work Item. Deliberately writes no Work Item field — status belongs to the Software Development process.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f5</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f6</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:scriptTask>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="f6" sourceRef="answer" targetRef="end" />
    <bpmn:endEvent id="end" name="Orchestrated">
      <bpmn:incoming>f6</bpmn:incoming>
    </bpmn:endEvent>
    <bpmn:adHocSubProcess id="orchestrator_tools" name="Tools (A2A specialists)">
      <bpmn:documentation>The specialists the orchestrator may delegate to. One shape per specialist — adding another is a shape plus a tick of "Exposed over A2A" on that agent.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f_call_tools</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f_tools_back</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="wi_relates_to" name="What this relates to" spiffworkflow:serverScript="Work Item Tool: What This Relates To" spiffworkflow:scriptType="Server Script" spiffworkflow:scriptName="Work Item Tool: What This Relates To" spiffworkflow:aiToolParams="{&#34;properties&#34;: {}, &#34;required&#34;: []}">
        <bpmn:documentation>Look up what this work item relates to — its epic, the other items in its sprint, and anything filed under it. Use it when the work only makes sense next to its neighbours: sizing something, spotting that another item already covers it, or checking what the epic is actually for. The work item's own text is already in your prompt; this is for everything around it.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:script>pass</bpmn:script>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="wi_comment" name="Record a comment" spiffworkflow:serverScript="Work Item Tool: Record a Comment" spiffworkflow:scriptType="Server Script" spiffworkflow:scriptName="Work Item Tool: Record a Comment" spiffworkflow:aiToolParams="{&#34;properties&#34;: {&#34;comment&#34;: {&#34;type&#34;: &#34;string&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;What to record, in plain words. One note, not a running log.&#34;}}, &#34;required&#34;: [&#34;comment&#34;]}">
        <bpmn:documentation>Write a note onto the work item so it survives this run. Use it for something a person will want later — a decision you took, a limitation you hit, what you checked. Not for narrating progress: your final report already reaches them.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:script>pass</bpmn:script>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="wi_hand_back" name="Hand back to a person" spiffworkflow:serverScript="Work Item Tool: Hand Back to a Person" spiffworkflow:scriptType="Server Script" spiffworkflow:scriptName="Work Item Tool: Hand Back to a Person" spiffworkflow:aiToolParams="{&#34;properties&#34;: {&#34;assignee&#34;: {&#34;type&#34;: &#34;string&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;Optional. The user to hand it to, as their login email. Leave it out unless the work item names somebody specific \u2014 the default is the person who raised it.&#34;}, &#34;reason&#34;: {&#34;type&#34;: &#34;string&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;Why it needs a person. Recorded on the item so they know what they are picking up.&#34;}}, &#34;required&#34;: [&#34;reason&#34;]}">
        <bpmn:documentation>Give the work back to a person and stop being its owner. Use it when the work needs a human — a decision only they can take, access you do not have, or something outside what any specialist here can do. You do NOT need to know who: leave the assignee out and it goes to the person who raised the work item, who can send it onward. Clears the orchestrator flag in the same save, so the assignment actually sticks. If you conclude a person must do this, call this tool — saying so in your report hands it to nobody.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:script>pass</bpmn:script>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="wi_set_state" name="Move the state" spiffworkflow:serverScript="Work Item Tool: Move the State" spiffworkflow:scriptType="Server Script" spiffworkflow:scriptName="Work Item Tool: Move the State" spiffworkflow:aiToolParams="{&#34;properties&#34;: {&#34;state&#34;: {&#34;type&#34;: &#34;string&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;The state to move to. It must be one this tool permits; you will be told the list if you get it wrong.&#34;}}, &#34;required&#34;: [&#34;state&#34;]}" spiffworkflow:allowedStates="Open,In Progress,Changes Requested">
        <bpmn:documentation>Move the work item to one of a short list of states this tool is allowed to set. It cannot set anything outside that list — the process owns the rest, including Done. If the state you want is refused, that is a deliberate limit and not something to retry.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:script>pass</bpmn:script>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:serviceTask id="delegate_connector_agent" name="A2A → Connector Agent" spiffworkflow:serviceType="connector" spiffworkflow:connectorId="a2a" spiffworkflow:operation="delegate_to_local_agent" spiffworkflow:connectorParams="{&#34;agent&#34;: &#34;Connector Agent&#34;, &#34;instruction&#34;: &#34;{{ task_data.instruction }}&#34;, &#34;work_item&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.name }}&#34;, &#34;pull_request&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.pr_link }}&#34;, &#34;target_app&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.target_app }}&#34;, &#34;git_branch&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.git_branch }}&#34;}" spiffworkflow:resultVariable="connector_result" spiffworkflow:aiToolParams="{&#34;properties&#34;: {&#34;instruction&#34;: {&#34;type&#34;: &#34;string&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;A self-contained work order for the connector builder: which provider&#39;s API to integrate, what the process needs to call, and the documentation or OpenAPI URL if the work item named one. It can read the work item and the pull request itself, so this is your framing - what to do and any decision you took; for a change request, name the exact review comments to fix.&#34;}}, &#34;required&#34;: [&#34;instruction&#34;]}">
        <bpmn:documentation>Hand the job to the Connector Agent, which builds a Processa connector — base URL, auth type and one operation per API method — from a plain-words work order. Use this ONLY to create or extend an integration with an EXTERNAL third-party API that Processa does not yet talk to. Do NOT use it to change existing Python in the codebase — a seed patch, a handler module, a connector manifest or its declared fields are all server-side code and belong to the Dev Agent, even though the word 'connector' appears in them. It writes the connector disabled and reports what a person must still do.</bpmn:documentation>
      </bpmn:serviceTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="ask_story_owner" name="Ask the story owner" spiffworkflow:assigneeDocfield="reporter_user" spiffworkflow:taskActions="Answer" spiffworkflow:assignmentMode="Document Field" spiffworkflow:aiToolParams="{&#34;properties&#34;: {&#34;question&#34;: {&#34;type&#34;: &#34;string&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;The single ambiguous point, in plain words. One question, not a list.&#34;}, &#34;interpretations&#34;: {&#34;type&#34;: &#34;string&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;The readings you are choosing between, so the owner can pick one instead of writing an essay.&#34;}}, &#34;required&#34;: [&#34;question&#34;, &#34;interpretations&#34;]}">
        <bpmn:documentation>Ask the story owner a question and WAIT for their answer. Use this when the work item leaves a requirement genuinely open — missing, self-contradictory, or readable more than one way — and you cannot settle it from the text you were given. State the one specific point you are stuck on and the readings you are choosing between, so it can be answered in a sentence. Do not use it for things you can reasonably decide yourself, and do not use it to confirm work you have already done.</bpmn:documentation>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:serviceTask id="delegate_mobile_app_agent" name="A2A → Mobile App Agent" spiffworkflow:serviceType="connector" spiffworkflow:connectorId="a2a" spiffworkflow:operation="delegate_to_local_agent" spiffworkflow:connectorParams="{&#34;agent&#34;: &#34;Mobile App Agent&#34;, &#34;instruction&#34;: &#34;{{ task_data.instruction }}&#34;, &#34;work_item&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.name }}&#34;, &#34;pull_request&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.pr_link }}&#34;, &#34;target_app&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.target_app }}&#34;, &#34;git_branch&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.git_branch }}&#34;}" spiffworkflow:resultVariable="connector_result" spiffworkflow:aiToolParams="{&#34;properties&#34;: {&#34;instruction&#34;: {&#34;type&#34;: &#34;string&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;A self-contained work order for the mobile app agent: which screen or behaviour to change, what it should do afterwards, and any endpoint or field the work item named. It can read the work item and the pull request itself, so this is your framing - what to do and any decision you took; for a change request, name the exact review comments to fix.&#34;}}, &#34;required&#34;: [&#34;instruction&#34;]}">
        <bpmn:documentation>Hand the job to the Mobile App Agent, which changes the ONE-F-M Ionic mobile app — screens, stores, API modules, routes and translations — in an isolated sandbox, runs the repository's checks, and delivers the change as a pull request for a person to review. Use this when the work item asks for a feature or a bug fix in the mobile app. It only does the mobile half: if the change also needs a new backend endpoint in one_fm, it will say so rather than invent one. It does not build the native app, so it never claims a screen looks right on a device.</bpmn:documentation>
      </bpmn:serviceTask>
      <bpmn:serviceTask id="delegate_frontend_agent" name="A2A → Frontend Agent" spiffworkflow:serviceType="connector" spiffworkflow:connectorId="a2a" spiffworkflow:operation="delegate_to_local_agent" spiffworkflow:connectorParams="{&#34;agent&#34;: &#34;Frontend Agent&#34;, &#34;instruction&#34;: &#34;{{ task_data.instruction }}&#34;, &#34;work_item&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.name }}&#34;, &#34;pull_request&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.pr_link }}&#34;, &#34;target_app&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.target_app }}&#34;, &#34;git_branch&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.git_branch }}&#34;}" spiffworkflow:resultVariable="connector_result" spiffworkflow:aiToolParams="{&#34;properties&#34;: {&#34;instruction&#34;: {&#34;type&#34;: &#34;string&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;A self-contained work order for the frontend agent: which screen, form or route to change, what it should do afterwards, and the DocType or path if the work item named one. It can read the work item and the pull request itself, so this is your framing - what to do and any decision you took; for a change request, name the exact review comments to fix.&#34;}}, &#34;required&#34;: [&#34;instruction&#34;]}">
        <bpmn:documentation>Hand the job to the Frontend Agent, which changes the front end of this bench — Vue screens in the Processa application, and Frappe desk JavaScript for forms and list views — and delivers the change as a pull request for a person to review. Use this for ANY change to a screen, a form, a field's behaviour on a form, a desk view, or a .vue or desk .js file, even when the work item calls it a code change. It works out which app owns the screen: ours it edits directly, and for an upstream DocType like an ERPNext one it writes the script in one_fm and registers it against that DocType instead. It works in an isolated sandbox and runs the app's real test suite, but it does not open a browser, so it never claims a screen looks right, and it writes nothing to any site.</bpmn:documentation>
      </bpmn:serviceTask>
      <bpmn:serviceTask id="delegate_dev_agent" name="A2A → Dev Agent" spiffworkflow:serviceType="connector" spiffworkflow:connectorId="a2a" spiffworkflow:operation="delegate_to_local_agent" spiffworkflow:connectorParams="{&#34;agent&#34;: &#34;Dev Agent&#34;, &#34;instruction&#34;: &#34;{{ task_data.instruction }}&#34;, &#34;work_item&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.name }}&#34;, &#34;pull_request&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.pr_link }}&#34;, &#34;target_app&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.target_app }}&#34;, &#34;git_branch&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.git_branch }}&#34;}" spiffworkflow:resultVariable="dev_agent_result" spiffworkflow:aiToolParams="{&#34;properties&#34;: {&#34;instruction&#34;: {&#34;type&#34;: &#34;string&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;A self-contained work order for the Dev Agent: which app to change, the branch to start from, and exactly what needs to be built or fixed. It can read the work item and the pull request itself, so this is your framing - what to do and any decision you took; for a change request, name the exact review comments to fix.&#34;}}, &#34;required&#34;: [&#34;instruction&#34;]}">
        <bpmn:documentation>Hand the job to the Dev Agent, which clones the target app into an isolated Cloud Run sandbox, makes the change, runs the app's real test suite, and delivers a passing change as a pull request — or reports exactly what stopped it. Use this ONLY for server-side code in an installed app: Python, DocTypes, API endpoints, background jobs. Do NOT use it for a screen, form, field behaviour or desk view (that is the Frontend Agent), for the Ionic mobile app (Mobile App Agent), for a new external-API integration (Connector Agent), or for a confirmed, reproducible defect (that is the Bug Agent, which reproduces it with a failing test before touching anything else). A change to a .vue or a desk .js file is front-end work even when it is also code.</bpmn:documentation>
      </bpmn:serviceTask>
      <bpmn:serviceTask id="delegate_bug_agent" name="A2A → Bug Agent" spiffworkflow:serviceType="connector" spiffworkflow:connectorId="a2a" spiffworkflow:operation="delegate_to_local_agent" spiffworkflow:connectorParams="{&#34;agent&#34;: &#34;Bug Agent&#34;, &#34;instruction&#34;: &#34;{{ task_data.instruction }}&#34;, &#34;work_item&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.name }}&#34;, &#34;pull_request&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.pr_link }}&#34;, &#34;target_app&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.target_app }}&#34;, &#34;git_branch&#34;: &#34;{{ doc.git_branch }}&#34;}" spiffworkflow:resultVariable="bug_agent_result" spiffworkflow:aiToolParams="{&#34;properties&#34;: {&#34;instruction&#34;: {&#34;type&#34;: &#34;string&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;A self-contained work order for the Bug Agent: the exact symptom, how to trigger it, and which app and branch it lives on. It can read the work item and the pull request itself, so this is your framing - what to do and any decision you took; for a change request, name the exact review comments to fix.&#34;}}, &#34;required&#34;: [&#34;instruction&#34;]}">
        <bpmn:documentation>Hand the job to the Bug Agent, which reproduces a defect with a failing test in an isolated Cloud Run sandbox BEFORE changing anything else, then fixes it and confirms the target app's real test suite passes — or reports that it could not reproduce the symptom. Use this for a CONFIRMED defect in server-side code (Python, DocTypes, API endpoints, background jobs) where the reported behaviour is wrong and reproducible — not for a new feature, a vague or unconfirmed report, a screen/form/desk-JS change (Frontend Agent), or the Ionic mobile app (Mobile App Agent). Prefer the Dev Agent instead for a feature, a small change, or a fix where reproducing it first with a test is not the point. If it cannot reproduce the symptom, it says so rather than guessing at a fix.</bpmn:documentation>
      </bpmn:serviceTask>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="get_pull_request" name="Read the pull request" spiffworkflow:serverScript="GitHub Tool: Read Pull Request" spiffworkflow:scriptType="Server Script" spiffworkflow:scriptName="GitHub Tool: Read Pull Request" spiffworkflow:aiToolParams="{&#34;properties&#34;: {&#34;pr_url&#34;: {&#34;type&#34;: &#34;string&#34;, &#34;description&#34;: &#34;Optional. The pull request URL; defaults to the one this task is about.&#34;}}, &#34;required&#34;: []}">
        <bpmn:documentation>Read-only. The pull request's description, changed files and every review comment, the ones that asked for changes first. Call it FIRST when the work item is in Changes Requested and names a pull request.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:script>GitHub Tool: Read Pull Request</bpmn:script>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
    </bpmn:adHocSubProcess>
  </bpmn:process>
  <bpmndi:BPMNDiagram id="BPMNDiagram_orchestrator_agent">
    <bpmndi:BPMNPlane id="BPMNPlane_orchestrator_agent" bpmnElement="orchestrator_agent">
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="start_di" bpmnElement="start">
        <dc:Bounds x="160" y="180" width="36" height="36" />
        <bpmndi:BPMNLabel>
          <dc:Bounds x="139" y="223" width="79" height="27" />
        </bpmndi:BPMNLabel>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="read_brief_di" bpmnElement="read_brief">
        <dc:Bounds x="250" y="158" width="100" height="80" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="gw_merge_di" bpmnElement="gw_merge" isMarkerVisible="true">
        <dc:Bounds x="400" y="173" width="50" height="50" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="orchestrate_di" bpmnElement="orchestrate">
        <dc:Bounds x="500" y="158" width="100" height="80" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="gw_needs_tools_di" bpmnElement="gw_needs_tools" isMarkerVisible="true">
        <dc:Bounds x="650" y="173" width="50" height="50" />
        <bpmndi:BPMNLabel>
          <dc:Bounds x="633" y="130" width="85" height="27" />
        </bpmndi:BPMNLabel>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="answer_di" bpmnElement="answer">
        <dc:Bounds x="750" y="158" width="100" height="80" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="end_di" bpmnElement="end">
        <dc:Bounds x="900" y="180" width="36" height="36" />
        <bpmndi:BPMNLabel>
          <dc:Bounds x="891" y="223" width="55" height="14" />
        </bpmndi:BPMNLabel>
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="orchestrator_tools_di" bpmnElement="orchestrator_tools" isExpanded="true">
        <dc:Bounds x="340" y="440" width="820" height="460" />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="wi_relates_to_di" bpmnElement="wi_relates_to">
        <dc:Bounds x="390" y="620" width="100" height="80" />
        <bpmndi:BPMNLabel />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="wi_comment_di" bpmnElement="wi_comment">
        <dc:Bounds x="520" y="620" width="100" height="80" />
        <bpmndi:BPMNLabel />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
      <bpmndi:BPMNShape id="wi_hand_back_di" bpmnElement="wi_hand_back">
        <dc:Bounds x="650" y="620" width="100" height="80" />
        <bpmndi:BPMNLabel />
      </bpmndi:BPMNShape>
